<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  One entitlement, and it is not optional.

  The choice to fetch ONNX Runtime after install rather than
  bundle it sidesteps `externalBin` and Frameworks-dylib signing. Phase 0
  spike 1 measured what that costs: every official
  ONNX Runtime release is ad-hoc signed with no Team Identifier, and a
  hardened-runtime process signed with ours refuses to map it -

      code signature in '…/libonnxruntime.dylib' not valid for use in process:
      mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs

  Library validation has to be off for the downloaded runtime to load at all.
  This weakens the process for every dylib, not only that one, which is a
  trade made without pricing. If the runtime is ever bundled and signed with
  our own identity instead, this file should go away - spike 1's case 4 shows
  that configuration needs no entitlement.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
